<p class="lead">Home service businesses lose money in small, repeated ways: a missed call, a no-show, an invoice sent a week late. Home service business automation fixes these one at a time. Here is the order we'd tackle them in, starting with what pays back fastest.</p>
<h2 id="missed-calls">1. Missed calls</h2>
<p>When you can't pick up, the caller usually tries the next company. An automatic text back within a minute ("Sorry we missed you, how can we help?") keeps the conversation going. A step further is a voice agent that answers, takes details and books a slot. Text messages and calls run through a provider such as <a href="/integrations/twilio">Twilio</a>.</p>
<h2 id="booking-confirmations-and-reminders">2. Booking confirmations and reminders</h2>
<p>A confirmation when the job is booked, a reminder the day before and an "on my way" text from the tech. These cut no-shows and "when are you coming?" calls to the office.</p>
<h2 id="invoices-and-payment-links">3. Invoices and payment links</h2>
<p>Send the invoice from the job the moment it's marked complete, with a card payment link through <a href="/integrations/stripe">Stripe</a>. Sync paid invoices to <a href="/integrations/quickbooks-online">QuickBooks</a> so nobody retypes them.</p>
<h2 id="job-photos-to-google-posts">4. Job photos to Google posts</h2>
<p>Your techs already photograph their work. Turn those photos into Google Business Profile posts with an approval step. Our <a href="/blogs/google-business-profile-hvac-job-photos">HVAC job photo guide</a> explains how, and what it did for one company.</p>
<h2 id="review-requests">5. Review requests</h2>
<p>A review request sent a few hours after a good job, with a direct link, gets far more reviews than asking at the door. Send it only for completed jobs, and route unhappy replies to the owner instead.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-start-without-breaking-anything">How to start without breaking anything</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Write down how one job flows today, from first call to payment.</li>
  <li>Pick the single step that costs you the most money or time.</li>
  <li>Automate that step around your current software first.</li>
  <li>Measure it for a month before moving to the next one.</li>
</ul>
<p>See what we build for HVAC, plumbing and other trades on our <a href="/solutions/home-services">home services page</a>.</p>
